- op: discuss  # 7342
  at: '2026-08-11T11:05:11Z'  # 7342
  by: agent  # 7342
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7342
  clock: 9  # 7342
  nonce: '7342'  # 7342
  message: "SATISFIED for the runtime entry points, with behavioural evidence.\n\nWHAT LANDED. New safety_zones.user_out_mkdir, and eight callsites migrated:\n  cli.py:1033  cmd_sketch            shutil.rmtree -> tmp_artifact_rmtree\n  cli.py:1450  _handle_files_mode    Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir\n  cli.py:1942  cmd_slice             Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir\n  cli.py:4490  cmd_compact           Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir\n  cli.py:9451  _emit_handler_slices  Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir\n  cli.py:10227 run_survey            Path.mkdir    -> user_out_mkdir\n  sketch_embeddings.py:1431/1472     Path.mkdir    -> cache_mkdir\nuser_out_mkdir registered in docs/hypergumbo-self-catalog/user_out_sinks.yaml so\nthe zone-barrier sanitizer applies. cache_mkdir and tmp_artifact_rmtree already\nexisted and were simply not being used.\n\nWHY THE WRAPPER WAS MISSING RATHER THAN MERELY UNUSED. safety_zones had\ncache_mkdir, tmp_artifact_mkdir and install_artifact_mkdir but no user_out_mkdir\n-- the one zone whose path comes from the USER (--out) was the one zone with a\nwrite wrapper and no mkdir wrapper, so the correct call did not exist to make.\nFour of the five cli.py mkdirs sit on the line DIRECTLY ABOVE a user_out_write /\nuser_out_open_json_dump call: the write was wrapped, the mkdir creating its\ndirectory could not be.\n\nuser_out_mkdir is deliberately NOT zone-bounded, unlike cache_rmtree /\ntmp_artifact_rmtree. There is no zone root to check against -- the user names the\npath. The wrapper buys ATTRIBUTION, not restriction: the barrier stops the taint\nwalk so the write records as a user_out crossing rather than an unattributed\nhost_fs flow. Creating the directory the user asked for is correct behaviour.\n\nMEASURED -- AND THE FLOWS DID NOT VANISH, THEY BECAME ATTRIBUTED. This is the\ndistinction that separates a fix from laundering, so it is quoted in full:\n  BEFORE (dev 528a7e5bcb):  violated, 370 raw / 11 distinct unsanitized\n                            (10 mkdir + 1 rmtree)\n  AFTER:                    inconclusive, 0 unsanitized, and verify-claims'\n                            own details reads \"2190 flow(s) reach that zone but\n                            pass through a sanitizer on every route\"\nThe writes are still detected and still reach host_fs. They are now sanitized via\ndeclared wrappers instead of surfacing unattributed.\n\nINCONCLUSIVE, NOT CONFIRMED, and that is correct rather than a shortfall. The\nverdict text: \"NOT CONFIRMED: this repo contains code in language(s) with no\ntaint catalogue (bash, csharp, solidity), so the analysis could not look for the\nflows this claim forbids.\" That is the WI-kajil/INV-bitig honest-verdict gate\ndoing its job -- the same reason 16 of the 18 claims are inconclusive. Confirming\na negative needs completeness the analysis cannot establish here.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE (closure-evidence discipline -- this item's statement is\nabout runtime behaviour, so a proxy metric cannot substitute). The item's own\nfiled repro, re-run against the merged tree:\n  survey . --out <TMP>/ARBITRARY2/deep/nested/tree/map.json\n    -> all four directory levels created, map.json + tier outputs written, rc=0\n  sketch . with /tmp/hypergumbo_sketch_compare/leftover/old.md pre-seeded\n    -> legacy dir drained, rc=0\nBoth features preserved. The rmtree is now ALSO zone-enforced (raises outside the\nsystem temp dir), which is strictly stronger than the bare call it replaced. The\nbare call carried ignore_errors=True, which the wrapper does not accept; the\nfail-open intent is preserved explicitly so a stale directory owned by another\nuser cannot abort sketch.\n\nENFORCED AS THE RULE, NOT THE EIGHT INSTANCES. New test file\ntest_runtime_fs_write_wrapper_discipline.py walks the runtime modules' ASTs for\ndirect calls to catalogued fs_write primitives, deriving the name set from the\nlive catalogue so a new primitive extends the guard automatically. It reuses the\ncatalogue's OWN curated ambiguous_names to drop bare-name collisions -- without\nthat filter it flagged 21 false positives against the 8 real ones\n(sym.id.replace(prefix, \"\") x13, sys.stderr.write x3, yaml.dump), which is the\nINV-tapat/INV-maluk shape appearing in a test rather than in production.\n\nSTATED LIMIT, not left implicit: an unwrapped os.remove on the runtime path is\nNOT caught, because `remove` is ambiguous. A null from that file means \"no\nunambiguous direct write\", not \"no direct write\"; the boundary analysis, which\nhas receiver evidence, covers the rest.\n\nSNAPSHOT: docs/hypergumbo.claims.expected.json ratifies exactly one line,\nruntime-cli-no-host-fs violated -> inconclusive. Nothing moved toward violated."  # 7342
- op: discuss  # 3881
  at: '2026-08-20T08:35:35Z'  # 3881
  by: agent  # 3881
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3881
  clock: 10  # 3881
  nonce: '3881'  # 3881
  message: "REGRESSION AGAINST THIS ITEM'S STATEMENT, second reopen-shaped finding. Filed here\nrather than as a sibling INV because it is a property of this statement, not a new\nrule: \"every entry point with an asserted no-direct-host_fs claim must route every\nfs-write primitive through a safety_zones.py wrapper.\"\n\nTHE INSTANCE. runtime-cli-no-host-fs was VIOLATED at dev 87424631da with 2\nunsanitized flows, both through cache eviction:\n  cmd_run          -> _maybe_evict_cache -> _archive_entry -> pathlib.Path.rename\n  cmd_cache_status -> _maybe_evict_cache -> _archive_entry -> pathlib.Path.rename\nsafety_zones shipped NO rename wrapper of any zone, so those two calls were bare\nfor want of anything to call. Fixed in PR #435 (dev 9046ff4255) by adding\ncache_rename, which guards BOTH endpoints -- an in-zone source can still deposit\nbytes anywhere on the host, the one way a rename guard differs in shape from every\nsingle-path wrapper here.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE, reconciled to a total (closure-evidence discipline; this\nstatement is about runtime behaviour). check-self-claims on the merged tree exits 0\nwith the snapshot UNCHANGED -- the recorded verdict is restored, not redefined:\n  unsanitized flows  2 -> 0\n  sanitized flows   85 -> 87\n85 + 2 = 87, so the flows are neutralised, not deleted. That distinguishes this\nfrom the #214 shape where an unearned False removes a finding.\n\nWHY BOTH GUARD LAYERS WERE OPEN AT ONCE -- the part worth more than the bug.\nThe 2026-08-11 closure of this item said \"ENFORCED AS THE RULE, NOT THE EIGHT\nINSTANCES\", via test_runtime_fs_write_wrapper_discipline.py, which derives its\nprimitive set from the live catalogue \"so a new primitive extends the guard\nautomatically\". It did not catch this, and it was not broken:\n  - rename IS catalogued fs_write (pathlib.Path.rename AND os.rename), but it is\n    in catalog.ambiguous_names, so that guard subtracts it. That is deliberate and\n    documented -- the guard calls itself a syntactic backstop for the unambiguous\n    majority and delegates the rest to \"the boundary analysis, which has receiver\n    evidence\".\n  - The boundary analysis is the self-claims-gate CI step. It is path-triggered on\n    the claim surface (**/pyproject.toml, the claims files, io_primitives/taint_*)\n    and had NOT RUN FOR 63 COMMITS (last: e6cb77fa15). It fired only because a\n    version bump touched every pyproject.toml.\nSo layer 1 delegated its blind spot to a layer that was not running. The\nregression was introduced by claim-surface work in this same cycle (rowing\npathlib.Path's surface) and then survived 63 commits behind a green pipeline.\n\nSTILL OPEN, not addressed by PR #435:\n  (a) replace and remove sit in exactly the same position -- catalogued fs_write,\n      ambiguous, therefore invisible to layer 1 and dependent on a gate that runs\n      only on path-trigger. The next ambiguous fs-write primitive lands there too.\n  (b) A gate whose trigger is a path set cannot see a regression that its own\n      subject matter introduced elsewhere. A scheduled arm alongside the path\n      trigger would have caught this in a day rather than 63 commits. This may\n      deserve its own item; flagging rather than filing, per the\n      conversation-in-progress rule -- the owner is in this conversation.\n\nPR #435 also extends the parity test's naming rule from (_rmtree, _unlink) to\ninclude _rename. Its docstring already promised \"a newly added one cannot ship\nunenforced\", and a _rename wrapper would have walked straight past it.\n\nNOT changing status. This item is satisfied on evidence that was true for the\ninstances it enumerated, and it has three prior closures; whether a fourth\nsibling-primitive finding reopens it is an owner call, not mine to take\nmid-release."  # 3881
