- op: discuss  # 1e1e
  at: '2026-08-20T18:44:18Z'  # 1e1e
  by: agent  # 1e1e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1e1e
  clock: 12  # 1e1e
  nonce: 1e1e  # 1e1e
  message: "REOPENED off satisfied at owner request (in-session), to pending_validation\nrather than violated -- and the distinction is evidence, not hedging.\n\nWHY NOT violated. The item's property currently HOLDS. Measured on dev\n4799c634e9, not assumed: an AST scan of the three runtime modules (cli.py,\nsketch_embeddings.py, finalize.py) finds no unwrapped fs-write primitive. Every\n.replace call is str.replace -- two arguments; Path.replace takes one -- and\nthere is no os.remove, os.rename, os.replace or shutil.move in any of them.\ncheck-self-claims exits 0 with 18/18 confirmed_with_caveats and the snapshot\nUNCHANGED. Filing violated would assert a state I had just disproved, which is\nthe closure-evidence rule running in the other direction.\n\nWHY NOT satisfied either. A fix WAS deployed this cycle and has NOT been\nvalidated by bakeoff -- nothing has run against real repos in about a month --\nwhich is precisely what pending_validation means.\n\nWHAT THE FIX WAS. PR #435 (dev 9046ff4255) adds safety_zones.cache_rename and\nroutes _archive_entry's two bare Path.rename calls through it. Both endpoints\nare guarded: an in-zone source can still deposit bytes anywhere on the host, the\none way a rename's guard differs in shape from every single-path wrapper in that\nmodule. Flows reconciled to a total: unsanitized 2 -> 0, sanitized 85 -> 87.\n85 + 2 = 87, so the flows are neutralised rather than deleted -- not the #214\nshape where an unearned False removes a finding.\n\nWHAT IS STILL OPEN, and why this item should not close on the rename alone: the\nenforcement named in the 2026-08-11 closure (\"ENFORCED AS THE RULE, NOT THE\nEIGHT INSTANCES\") structurally cannot see three of the seven primitives this\nitem's own statement enumerates. rename, replace and remove are all catalogued\nfs_write AND in catalog.ambiguous_names, so test_runtime_fs_write_wrapper_\ndiscipline.py filters them out by design and delegates them to the self-claims\ngate -- which is path-triggered and had not run for 63 commits when this\nregression landed. That delegation is now filed as its own item, INV-bobor,\nbecause it is a property of the GATE rather than of this invariant.\n\nPR #435 closes rename by name with an AST guard, and extends the parity test's\nnaming rule from (_rmtree, _unlink) to include _rename -- that test's docstring\nalready promised \"a newly added one cannot ship unenforced\" and a _rename\nwrapper would have walked straight past it. replace and remove remain uncovered\nby name; the exposure is latent (nothing unwrapped today), not live.\n\nTO CLOSE: bakeoff validation, plus either INV-bobor landing or a name-level\nguard for the remaining two ambiguous primitives."  # 1e1e
